# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE COLLECTION IS NOT TRACKED. This is deliberate and load-bearing.
#
# items/ holds third-party source — paid templates, ripped components, code with
# licences that permit personal reference but not redistribution. Committing it
# would republish someone else's work under this repo. It also holds the capture
# media (posters, video, textures, .glb), which runs to gigabytes.
#
# The tool is the open part. The morgue is yours alone.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
items/*
!items/.gitkeep

# Same reason, one level up: archives/ holds the ingested deliveries themselves —
# whole paid templates with their source, node_modules and built export. An item
# that lives in an archive is a few KB of JSON pointing into it, and the thing it
# points at is exactly the third-party code items/ already refuses to track.
archives/*
!archives/.gitkeep

# Ingest working-state: the manifests, dedup ledgers and batch lists `pnpm
# ingest`/`ingest-batch` write while carving a delivery drop into items/. It
# names the paid deliveries by folder and zip and records the local
# `~/Downloads/…` paths they were extracted from — collection metadata, not
# tool. The scripts that produce it are tracked; the output is yours alone.
.ingest/

# Generated media and built output — reproducible from source via `pnpm capture`.
out/
site/
# `pnpm test` builds fixtures/ into its own tree rather than site/. The two used
# to share one directory, which meant a test run replaced the vault's index with
# 11 fixture records and — once build.mjs learned to prune — deleted every real
# item out of site/ as a side effect of running the tests.
site-fixtures/

# Fixture build artefacts. The SOURCE of every fixture is committed; the built
# output is not, so a stale export never ends up in git.
fixtures/**/bundle.js
fixtures/**/_next/
fixtures/**/.next/
fixtures/nextjs-static/index.html
fixtures/nextjs-static/404.html
fixtures/nextjs-static/_not-found*
fixtures/nextjs-static/*.txt

# Dependencies
node_modules/
.pnpm-store/

# Scratch
.det/
*.log
.DS_Store
.env
.env.*
# ...but the template must be tracked, or nobody knows what to fill in.
!.env.example

# ─── web/ ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The Next app. Source is tracked; build output and local secrets are not.
web/.next/
web/out/
web/next-env.d.ts

# Vercel CLI project link (contains project and org ids).
.vercel

# Local secrets. `vercel link` wrote `.env*` here, which also matches
# web/.env.example — tracked on purpose, because it is the documentation of what
# the app needs. An already-tracked file survives a gitignore rule, so that was
# latent rather than broken, and latent is exactly how it gets inherited.
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
